WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.510
Our school had a cyber safety policy.

2
00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:08.500
Twelve pages, in a folder, in the

3
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:09.980
head teacher’s cupboard.

4
00:00:10.770 --> 00:00:12.890
Nobody had read it, and

5
00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:14.350
I’m the cyber lead!

6
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.500
Today: how to write a one-page cyber

7
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.360
safety policy that your colleagues

8
00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.300
will actually read and follow.

9
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.070
A policy only protects the school

10
00:00:26.070 --> 00:00:28.010
if people know what it says.

11
00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.770
So keep it to one page, in plain words,

12
00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.870
with a name next to every job.

13
00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:36.810
Review it once a year, and

14
00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.130
whenever something goes wrong.

15
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:40.540
What goes on the page?

16
00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:42.700
Five short headings.

17
00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.430
Who leads: a named cyber

18
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:46.630
lead and a deputy.

19
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.100
Accounts: long passphrases, never

20
00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.640
shared, and two-step sign-in on email.

21
00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.450
Devices: screen locks,

22
00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:58.090
updates and approved apps.

23
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.040
Data and groups: collect less, share

24
00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:05.060
with named people, back it up, and give

25
00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.480
every official group two admins.

26
00:01:08.070 --> 00:01:10.910
Incidents: report the same day, and

27
00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.490
nobody is blamed for reporting.

28
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.290
Now, passwords: this is where

29
00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:17.770
old policies go wrong.

30
00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.080
Current guidance from NIST, the American

31
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:24.360
standards body, says length beats symbols.

32
00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:27.630
At least fifteen characters when a

33
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.990
password is used alone, or eight

34
00:01:29.990 --> 00:01:31.330
with two-step sign-in.

35
00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:35.270
No forced changes every month: change a

36
00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:37.150
password when there are signs it leaked.

37
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:39.720
And check new passwords against

38
00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:41.800
lists of common and leaked ones.

39
00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.620
Think of the timetable on

40
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:45.480
the staffroom wall.

41
00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:49.290
Everyone can see it, it fits on one sheet,

42
00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.110
and when it changes, everyone hears.

43
00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:54.530
Your policy should work the same way.

44
00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:56.160
Quick check!

45
00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:58.200
I’m drafting the password

46
00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.260
rule for our policy.

47
00:02:00.010 --> 00:02:01.050
Which one should I write?

48
00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:05.340
Long passphrases, changed only when

49
00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:06.360
there are signs of a leak.

50
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.880
Forced monthly changes push people to

51
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.260
add a one or an exclamation mark, and

52
00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.720
attackers know that trick.

53
00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:17.990
And a shared password can’t be traced,

54
00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:20.230
or taken back when someone leaves.

55
00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.350
A policy written alone in an

56
00:02:23.350 --> 00:02:25.350
office stays in that office.

57
00:02:26.110 --> 00:02:27.550
Draft it with three or four

58
00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.270
colleagues, the bursar included.

59
00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.340
Read it aloud at a staff meeting and

60
00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:34.680
ask: is anything here impossible?

61
00:02:35.510 --> 00:02:38.250
Then everyone signs it, and new staff

62
00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:39.690
sign it in their first week.

63
00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.450
Pupils get their own version: five

64
00:02:43.450 --> 00:02:46.610
promises in simple words, on the classroom

65
00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:46.970
wall.

66
00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:49.250
Parents hear the parts that touch

67
00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:51.510
them, like which groups are official,

68
00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.730
and that the school never asks for

69
00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.170
money or codes by message.

70
00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.260
Your turn.

71
00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.180
A colleague says: “The longer our

72
00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.360
policy, the safer we are.”

73
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:05.170
Agree or disagree?

74
00:03:06.810 --> 00:03:08.810
Disagree: rules nobody

75
00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.530
reads protect nobody.

76
00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.930
Remember: one page, a name for every job,

77
00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:17.390
password rules from current guidance,

78
00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:20.530
written together and reviewed every year.

79
00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:23.910
Try it at school tomorrow: ask

80
00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.710
three colleagues for the one

81
00:03:25.710 --> 00:03:27.110
rule they would put first.

82
00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.350
Their answers are the start of your page.
